-- title:
--   Surjectivity of the fine-level Čerednik–Drinfeld family on geometric points
-- statement:
--   The data and hypotheses fall into the following groups.
--
--   *Base arithmetic data.* Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a non-zero natural number $N$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring in which $2$ and $3$ are units, an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$ such that $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$; and a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   *Unramified coefficients and the determinant valuation.* A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $F_r$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial of positive degree over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$, and $F_r x \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (so the residue ring is an algebraic closure of $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ and $F_r$ lifts its Frobenius). A homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some $u \in \mathcal O^{\times}$.
--
--   *The indefinite algebra and the coarse moduli scheme.* Rationals $a, b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for each height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$; a scheme $\mathcal X$ with a morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O$ and an assignment $\mathrm{pt}$ sending each ring $S$, each morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and each fake elliptic curve over $S$ with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure to a point of $\mathcal X$ over $s$, such that `h𝒳` holds: $\mathrm{pt}$ is invariant under isomorphism, compatible with pullback along ring maps, bijective on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and universal among such assignments. Moreover the second projection of the pullback of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O \to K_0)$ — the generic fibre — is geometrically reduced and geometrically connected, and $f$ itself is geometrically connected (`hZ`).
--
--   *Fine moduli at full level $n$, twisting group, and the forgetful map.* A natural number $n \geq 3$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ and coprime to $N$; a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ via $f_M$ with an assignment $\mathrm{ptF}$ on fake elliptic curves with level-$N$ and full level-$n$ structure, such that `hM` holds: $\mathrm{ptF}$ is invariant under isomorphism, compatible with pullback, and bijective on $S$-points for every commutative ring $S$. A group $G$ with a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ and a labelling $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfying `hρ`: each $\rho(g)$ is a morphism over the base, $\rho(g)$ transports $\mathrm{ptF}$ along $\chi(g)$-twists of the full level structure, and the labels $\chi$ are multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n\Lambda$ in the sense of the four label clauses. A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $f_M$, invariant under $G$ (each $\rho(h)$ followed by $p$ is $p$), and compatible with the moduli assignments: $\mathrm{ptF}(S,s,u)$ followed by $p$ is $\mathrm{pt}(S,s,u_1)$.
--
--   *Hecke correspondences and Atkin–Lehner morphisms.* For every prime $\ell \notin \{r, \bar r\}$, a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with assignment $\mathrm{ptT}_\ell$ on curves carrying an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup, coarse for that problem (`h𝒴`), together with two morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base such that $d_0$ forgets the extra level structure and $d_1$ sends a point with extra level-$\ell$ structure to any $\ell$-isogenous quotient curve. Two endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base realising the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on moduli points.
--
--   *The definite quaternion side.* Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ a division algebra exactly at the places over $\bar r$ (`hdef`); a maximal order $\Lambda_1$ and an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$; an adelic unit $n_1$ in $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}\,R_1\,r$ such that $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$; the subgroup $\Gamma^{\sim} = \mathrm{awayUnits}\,R_1\,v$ of units that at every place $w \neq v$ lie in the subgroup generated by the local unit box of $R_1$ (`hΓt`). Elements $s(\ell)$ and adelic units $s_f(\ell)$, indexed by primes $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, satisfying `hs`: away from $r$ the local component of $s_f(\ell)$ is $s(\ell) \otimes 1$, at the places over $r$ it is $1$, the product of the diagonal image of the scalar $\ell$ with $s_f(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in $\mathrm{levelHeckeUSet}\,\Lambda_1\,(R_1 \cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1})\,\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}\,(R_1 \cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1})\,\ell$ otherwise, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s(\ell)) = \ell$; the subgroups $\Gamma^{\sim}_\ell = \Gamma^{\sim} \cap s(\ell)\Gamma^{\sim}s(\ell)^{-1}$; a unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma^{\sim}$, with $r^{k}\bar w \in R_1$ for some $k$; a unit $w_{\mathrm{odd}} \in \mathrm{awayUnits}\,R_1\,v$ of reduced norm $r$; and a homomorphism $\theta^{\sim} : \Gamma^{\sim} \to G$.
--
--   *The uniformisation map.* For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map
--   $$\Theta_f : \bigl((O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \widehat\Omega(B)\bigr) \times G \longrightarrow \{\,\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B \to M \text{ over } \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O\,\},$$
--   where $\widehat\Omega(B)$ is the set of Deligne data over $B$ (a $B$-line in $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} L$ for each full lattice $L \subset K_0^2$, with invertible quotient, monotone in $L$, equivariant for homothety, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$), subject to: `hnat`, naturality in $B$; `hG`, composing $\Theta_f(x, g h)$ with $\rho(h)$ gives $\Theta_f(x, g)$; `hinv`, if $\gamma \in \Gamma^{\sim}$ carries $x$ to $x'$ in the twisted sense (the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-component of $x'$ is the $F_r^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)}$-twist of that of $x$, and the datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\iota_0\gamma)^{-1}$), then $\Theta_f(x', \theta^{\sim}(\gamma) g) = \Theta_f(x, g)$; `het`, a unique-lifting property along every surjection $B \to B_0$ of such algebras whose kernel has square zero; and `hfib`, a description of the fibres over algebraically closed $k$: $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma \in \Gamma^{\sim}$ with $g' = \theta^{\sim}(\gamma) g$, with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $(\iota_0\gamma)^{-1}$, and with $\psi' y = \psi(F_r^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)} y)$ for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ that is fixed by $F_r^{\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0 z)}$ for all scalar $z \in \Gamma^{\sim}$ with $\theta^{\sim}(z) = 1$.
--
--   *Image and non-emptiness inputs.* An open subset $V$ of $M$ such that, for every point $x$ of $M$, the conjunction of $x \in V$ with the existence of an algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-algebra field $k$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and of a point $y : \operatorname{Spec} k \to M$ over the base whose closed point maps to $x$, is equivalent to the existence of such $k$ and $y$ whose closed point maps to $x$ and which lies in the image of $\Theta_f$, that is $y = \Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$ for some $\psi$, $P$, $g$ (`hV`). Finally `hne`: for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-algebra field $k$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, both $O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} k$ and $\widehat\Omega(k)$ are non-empty.
--
--   *Conclusion.* For every algebraically closed field $k$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, for every $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, and for every morphism $y : \operatorname{Spec} k \to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, there exist a Deligne datum $P \in \widehat\Omega(k)$ and an element $g \in G$ with $\Theta_f((\psi, P), g) = y$. Thus $\Theta_f$ is surjective on such points with the $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-component prescribed in advance.
--
--   This is the surjectivity statement for the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation of the fine-level Shimura curve attached to an indefinite quaternion algebra ramified at $r$ and $\bar r$: the formal family $\Theta_f$, built from the $\pi$-adic upper half plane, the unramified coefficients $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ and the arithmetic group $\Gamma^{\sim}$ coming from the definite algebra ramified at $\bar r$, reaches every geometric point of the special fibre, with the Frobenius component of the point prescribed. It is used in the assembly of the uniformisation isomorphism at fine level with squarefree level data, from which the reduction of the Shimura curve at $r$ is read off.
